00:02:26.630 --> 00:02:31.340
episode

00:02:26.630 --> 00:02:31.340
"His Smile is Lost Forever!"

00:02:37.090 --> 00:02:37.990
André!

00:02:37.990 --> 00:02:38.820
Hang in there!

00:02:38.820 --> 00:02:40.010
We're bringing you to a doctor!

00:02:48.330 --> 00:02:49.430
Keep pace!

00:02:49.430 --> 00:02:51.020
Charge through the middle!

00:03:05.410 --> 00:03:06.450
Get out of the way!

00:03:06.450 --> 00:03:07.660
Move, move, move!

00:03:09.250 --> 00:03:14.630
<i>The French Guards broke through the encircling forces while
protecting Alain's horse, {which was }carrying a gravely injured André.</i>

00:03:15.320 --> 00:03:17.020
<i>''I can't let André die.''</i>

00:03:17.590 --> 00:03:21.760
<i>André's bloody chest wound
had Oscar in a frenzied rage.</i>

00:03:21.760 --> 00:03:24.390
<i>Oscar charged forward as a hail
of bullets flew around her.</i>

00:03:24.770 --> 00:03:28.360
<i>''With André's life at stake, I have no fear!''</i>

00:03:44.250 --> 00:03:45.590
It's the French Guards!

00:03:45.590 --> 00:03:46.950
The French Guards have come back!

00:04:01.500 --> 00:04:02.050
André!

00:04:05.070 --> 00:04:07.950
Bernard, please get a doctor immediately!

00:04:11.480 --> 00:04:12.980
Hang in there, André!

00:04:12.980 --> 00:04:15.020
It's all right now! A doctor
will be here any minute!

00:04:16.220 --> 00:04:17.010
André!

00:04:19.050 --> 00:04:20.040
Everyone, please listen!

00:04:20.860 --> 00:04:24.780
André Grandier, a former French Guardsman
has returned with a serious wound!

00:04:25.430 --> 00:04:28.760
Anyone who has any medical knowledge,
please step forward!

00:04:29.260 --> 00:04:30.130
Time is of the essence!

00:04:30.130 --> 00:04:32.210
We want to save our comrade no matter what!

00:04:32.900 --> 00:04:35.200
I'm a practicing physician
in the Gravilliers district!

00:04:35.200 --> 00:04:36.420
Show me where this wounded man is!

00:04:36.770 --> 00:04:38.310
I'll help too!

00:04:38.310 --> 00:04:39.630
I'm a surgeon!

00:04:40.140 --> 00:04:42.090
<i>Not just 2,</i>

00:04:42.090 --> 00:04:46.850
<i>but over 10 doctors had stepped forward
from the crowd to help save André.</i>

00:04:46.850 --> 00:04:47.930
<i>However...</i>

00:04:53.660 --> 00:04:56.860
Is... Is the sun going down, Oscar?

00:04:56.860 --> 00:04:59.350
Yeah, today's battle is over.

00:04:59.960 --> 00:05:01.650
Not a single gunshot, right?

00:05:02.600 --> 00:05:06.410
I can hear the pigeons returning to their nests.

00:05:08.040 --> 00:05:08.900
Yes.

00:05:13.880 --> 00:05:15.830
How is André's wound?

00:05:16.380 --> 00:05:19.480
The bullet pierced straight through his heart.

00:05:19.480 --> 00:05:21.710
It's a wonder that he's still alive.

00:05:22.420 --> 00:05:25.050
I'm afraid there's nothing we can do.

00:05:33.390 --> 00:05:34.730
What's the matter, Oscar?

00:05:34.730 --> 00:05:36.360
Why are you crying?

00:05:36.360 --> 00:05:39.270
André, I want you to marry me...

00:05:39.700 --> 00:05:43.710
Once this battle is over,
take me some place far away...

00:05:43.710 --> 00:05:49.340
Let's find a small church somewhere in the
countryside, where we can have our wedding.

00:05:49.340 --> 00:05:53.240
I want you to vow before
God to make me your wife.

00:05:54.120 --> 00:05:55.350
Of course.

00:05:55.350 --> 00:05:57.250
That's what I had always planned, Oscar.

00:05:57.250 --> 00:05:58.330
I always had...

00:05:59.290 --> 00:06:01.960
But... Oscar, why the tears?

00:06:01.960 --> 00:06:02.800
Why are you crying?

00:06:03.860 --> 00:06:05.800
Am I... going to die?

00:06:09.500 --> 00:06:11.930
Don't be silly, André!

00:06:12.300 --> 00:06:13.300
You're right...

00:06:13.300 --> 00:06:15.770
Yeah, that can't be...

00:06:15.770 --> 00:06:18.170
Everything is about to begin...

00:06:18.170 --> 00:06:20.110
The love between us...

00:06:20.640 --> 00:06:22.840
The dawn of the new era...

00:06:22.840 --> 00:06:24.710
It's all going to begin now...

00:06:25.480 --> 00:06:27.740
I can't die at a time like this...

00:06:28.450 --> 00:06:30.140
I can't die...

00:06:34.220 --> 00:06:38.030
Long ago, when we went to Arras,
we saw the sun come up together.

00:06:38.030 --> 00:06:40.760
Let's go see that sunrise again, André.

00:06:41.300 --> 00:06:44.630
That beautiful morning sunshine...
Just the two of us...

00:06:44.630 --> 00:06:50.800
Let's count our blessings of being born, finding
each other and being able to live together...

00:06:56.280 --> 00:06:57.080
André?

00:06:57.480 --> 00:06:58.060
André?!

00:07:31.090 --> 00:07:33.050
André!

00:07:35.430 --> 00:07:37.930
You're leaving me behind?!

00:07:48.100 --> 00:07:49.720
André...!

00:07:58.240 --> 00:08:02.730
<i>André's body, along with the bodies of
the other French Guardsmen and citizens...</i>

00:08:02.730 --> 00:08:08.300
<i>...who had died in battle earlier that day,
was placed in a small church near the square.</i>

00:08:22.860 --> 00:08:27.480
Come to think of it, André never
played cards with us, did he?

00:08:27.800 --> 00:08:30.770
Yeah, he was a bit different.

00:08:30.770 --> 00:08:32.510
Hey, Alain. Want to play for me?

00:08:32.510 --> 00:08:34.450
I'm not in the mood to play today.

00:08:36.540 --> 00:08:37.910
Maybe some other time.

00:08:40.450 --> 00:08:42.040
What's the commander doing?

00:08:42.580 --> 00:08:43.910
Is she still in front of the church?

00:08:44.650 --> 00:08:45.520
Yeah.

00:08:54.330 --> 00:08:56.260
It's going to be chilly tonight.

00:08:58.030 --> 00:09:02.770
Commander, I know this sounds like a typical
thing to say, but André was a lucky guy.

00:09:03.500 --> 00:09:06.000
After all, you returned his love.

00:09:07.740 --> 00:09:09.370
Cheer up, all right?

00:09:10.180 --> 00:09:11.650
Alain, wait.

00:09:13.080 --> 00:09:16.050
I want you to take command of our
company starting tomorrow.

00:09:16.050 --> 00:09:17.530
I can't...

00:09:17.530 --> 00:09:19.790
I can't bring myself to lead
everyone anymore...

00:09:20.520 --> 00:09:23.750
Don't, Oscar! There's no end to it,
once you start saying things like that!

00:09:24.050 --> 00:09:27.660
It might not compare to your deep grief,

00:09:27.660 --> 00:09:31.500
but you're not the only
one who's grieving his loss.

00:09:33.840 --> 00:09:36.690
Just make sure you come see
us in the morning, all right?

00:09:36.690 --> 00:09:38.670
After all, this is where everything begins.

00:11:23.180 --> 00:11:25.990
It's the woman commander who led the
treasonous defection of the French Guards!

00:11:25.990 --> 00:11:27.070
Capture her!

00:11:44.220 --> 00:11:45.370
H-Hey!

00:11:45.880 --> 00:11:47.180
She's crying...!

00:12:08.200 --> 00:12:10.140
<i>I loved you, André...</i>

00:12:10.140 --> 00:12:12.440
<i>I probably always have...</i>

00:12:12.440 --> 00:12:14.340
<i>...but I realized it too late.</i>

00:12:15.850 --> 00:12:20.150
<i>If only I had realized I loved you sooner...</i>

00:12:20.930 --> 00:12:24.260
<i>I'm certain our days would
have been even brighter...</i>

00:12:24.930 --> 00:12:30.490
<i>You were beside me, so quietly and gently...</i>

00:12:31.140 --> 00:12:34.540
<i>I was unable to realize my love for you.</i>

00:12:35.070 --> 00:12:37.600
<i>André, please forgive me.</i>

00:12:38.240 --> 00:12:44.450
<i>Not realizing one's love is far
more sinful than betraying one's love.</i>

00:12:55.790 --> 00:12:58.690
<i>André, answer me...</i>

00:12:59.660 --> 00:13:02.760
<i>Is everything over now...?</i>

00:13:09.840 --> 00:13:10.570
Squad Leader!

00:13:11.410 --> 00:13:12.180
Squad Leader Alain!

00:13:12.940 --> 00:13:13.880
What is it?

00:13:13.880 --> 00:13:15.140
Commander Oscar disappeared!

00:13:15.450 --> 00:13:18.980
We've looked, but she's not in front of
the church or anywhere in the square!

00:13:18.980 --> 00:13:20.110
Um, what should we do?!

00:13:20.650 --> 00:13:22.450
You idiot, pull it together!

00:13:22.450 --> 00:13:24.120
She'll be back by morning.

00:13:24.120 --> 00:13:25.490
Don't worry!

00:13:25.490 --> 00:13:26.570
Make sure everyone knows that.

00:13:27.990 --> 00:13:30.320
Damn, it's starting to rain.

00:13:46.960 --> 00:13:48.500
Sorry, Father.

00:13:48.500 --> 00:13:49.930
Did that hurt?

00:13:49.930 --> 00:13:51.370
We're almost there.

00:13:51.370 --> 00:13:53.180
The Seine is just up ahead.

00:13:53.180 --> 00:13:59.140
You always used to tell me how you wanted
the Seine to take you away when you die.

00:13:59.140 --> 00:13:59.970
There.

00:14:22.400 --> 00:14:25.840
<i>''The Seine's flow cannot be stopped.''</i>

00:14:26.540 --> 00:14:28.800
<i>You always used to say that, Father...</i>

00:14:28.800 --> 00:14:32.210
<i>''The Seine continues to flow regardless.''</i>

00:14:32.210 --> 00:14:37.110
<i>''Swallowing up all the sadness and hardships,
the Seine continues to flow.''</i>

00:14:37.110 --> 00:14:40.220
<i>The night will last for a very a long time, but...</i>

00:14:40.220 --> 00:14:43.950
<i>''Eventually the sun will rise and in the bright morning sun,''</i>

00:14:43.950 --> 00:14:46.860
<i>''the weeping people will emerge from their doors.''</i>

00:14:47.190 --> 00:14:48.490
<i>And when they do...</i>

00:14:49.230 --> 00:14:53.750
<i>''The Seine will be flowing gently and
swiftly as it has always done.''</i>

00:14:55.230 --> 00:14:58.040
Shortly, the sun will rise on July 14.

00:15:00.970 --> 00:15:02.640
Alain, we've been waiting for you!

00:15:02.640 --> 00:15:05.380
I want you to listen in as the representative
of the French Guards.

00:15:06.210 --> 00:15:07.910
I'm not the representative.

00:15:07.910 --> 00:15:10.350
Our commander couldn't make it,
so I came in her stead.

00:15:10.750 --> 00:15:12.770
Sorry to call you in so suddenly.

00:15:12.770 --> 00:15:14.510
It's a matter that requires haste.

00:15:15.150 --> 00:15:16.480
Let me get straight to the point.

00:15:16.480 --> 00:15:20.440
We'll leave for Bastille Prison at daybreak,
and will unleash an attack!

00:15:20.890 --> 00:15:21.690
What?!

00:15:21.690 --> 00:15:22.900
The Bastille?!

00:15:23.350 --> 00:15:25.860
We received word late this evening.

00:15:25.860 --> 00:15:29.780
Bastille Prison

00:15:26.960 --> 00:15:32.630
A number of people saw large quantities of gunpowder
and cannonballs being carried into the Bastille yesterday.

00:15:32.630 --> 00:15:35.370
We kept a watchful eye and
noticed that despite this rain,

00:15:35.370 --> 00:15:38.660
the direction of the Bastille
cannons had been changed.

00:15:38.660 --> 00:15:40.170
The direction of the cannons?

00:15:40.170 --> 00:15:41.510
Indeed.

00:15:41.510 --> 00:15:44.620
Normally, they're aimed outwards,
but now they're aimed towards Paris!

00:15:44.620 --> 00:15:47.650
Meaning, they are aimed towards us, citizens!

00:15:47.650 --> 00:15:48.750
How despicable!

00:15:48.750 --> 00:15:50.750
They're planning to fire cannons at us!

00:15:50.750 --> 00:15:55.470
It's probably safe to assume that the King
has finally decided to wage war on us.

00:15:56.290 --> 00:15:59.930
We're currently contacting the groups
gathered in the other squares.

00:16:00.590 --> 00:16:03.960
Most likely, they'll all reach
the same conclusion as us.

00:16:03.960 --> 00:16:05.360
To bring down the Bastille!

00:16:06.260 --> 00:16:07.830
<i>Bastille Prison.</i>

00:16:07.830 --> 00:16:11.830
<i>It stood as yet another infamous
symbol of royal tyranny.</i>

00:16:12.400 --> 00:16:15.670
<i>The reason for that is because throughout
the long history of the French monarchy,</i>

00:16:15.670 --> 00:16:18.480
<i>those who sought freedom were sent
away to Bastille to be silenced.</i>

00:16:18.480 --> 00:16:23.540
<i>It was the prison where political and
ideological criminals were taken away.</i>

00:16:35.390 --> 00:16:38.520
<i>As daybreak grew closer, the rain had stopped.</i>

00:16:39.930 --> 00:16:44.840
<i>July 14, the day in which the French Revolution
was immortalized in history,</i>

00:16:44.840 --> 00:16:48.000
<i>was about to begin with the ''Storming of the Bastille.''</i>

00:16:48.820 --> 00:16:50.110
What?!

00:16:50.110 --> 00:16:51.690
Attack the Bastille?!

00:16:52.040 --> 00:16:55.700
Yes, that's the consensus the groups gathered
in all the squares have reached.

00:16:55.700 --> 00:16:57.280
A consensus?!

00:16:57.280 --> 00:16:59.050
Hold on, Bernard!

00:16:59.050 --> 00:17:01.160
I don't recall ever giving out such an order!

00:17:01.450 --> 00:17:04.220
Attacking the Bastille isn't part of my script!

00:17:04.220 --> 00:17:08.790
Monsieur, with all due respect,
a revolution cannot be scripted.

00:17:10.300 --> 00:17:12.230
Like the Seine dictates its flow,

00:17:12.230 --> 00:17:16.270
I believe we should let the people's hearts
dictate our path and actions.

00:17:16.730 --> 00:17:20.260
I figured I should have informed you,
but now I wish I hadn't.

00:17:20.910 --> 00:17:22.370
Please excuse me.

00:17:22.370 --> 00:17:23.600
We'll be leaving now.

00:17:25.240 --> 00:17:26.840
All right, I'll acknowledge it!

00:17:26.840 --> 00:17:28.650
I'll acknowledge your attack on the Bastille!

00:17:29.180 --> 00:17:30.510
But don't forget this!

00:17:30.510 --> 00:17:32.950
A revolution cannot succeed without a leader!

00:17:32.950 --> 00:17:33.880
Bernard!

00:17:34.550 --> 00:17:38.060
<i>The reason why the ''Storming of the
Bastille'' is so well known in history,</i>

00:17:38.060 --> 00:17:42.460
<i>is not only because it was a great battle
between the regime and its people,</i>

00:17:42.460 --> 00:17:47.300
<i>but because it was the first time the people
had come to a consensus and acted upon it.</i>

00:17:47.300 --> 00:17:50.900
<i>In other words, the true significance
of the ''Storming of the Bastille,''</i>

00:17:50.900 --> 00:17:54.440
<i>was that it wasn't led by the elite
revolutionists, such as Robespierre,</i>

00:17:54.440 --> 00:17:58.540
<i>but rather it was led by a
mass of unknown demonstrators,</i>

00:17:58.540 --> 00:18:03.980
<i>who truly yearned for the new era, that came
together in a moment of spontaneous unity.</i>

00:18:05.140 --> 00:18:08.590
<i>July 14, 1789.</i>

00:18:08.590 --> 00:18:12.080
<i>In essence, it was the day
the revolution had truly begun.</i>

00:18:23.170 --> 00:18:26.440
<i>Some demonstrators raided
the armory of the Invalides,</i>

00:18:26.440 --> 00:18:32.090
<i>and after obtaining 36,000 guns and 12 cannons,
they headed straight to Bastille.</i>

00:18:47.340 --> 00:18:48.220
The Bastille?

00:18:51.920 --> 00:18:52.900
André...!

00:18:53.450 --> 00:18:55.290
<i>Oscar, what's the matter?</i>

00:18:55.290 --> 00:18:56.650
<i>What are you doing here?</i>

00:18:57.290 --> 00:18:59.330
<i>Everyone is heading to the Bastille.</i>

00:18:59.330 --> 00:19:02.280
<i>The people have taken up guns and
are headed to the Bastille to fight!</i>

00:19:02.760 --> 00:19:06.750
<i>Your company men are still at the square though.</i>

00:19:06.750 --> 00:19:09.840
<i>They're waiting at the square,
trusting that you'll return!</i>

00:19:09.840 --> 00:19:14.500
Commander, they're all waiting for you
to return, so we can fight with you!

00:19:15.510 --> 00:19:16.130
Alain...

00:19:16.780 --> 00:19:18.220
To the Bastille!

00:19:21.220 --> 00:19:22.920
Thank you for this.

00:19:22.920 --> 00:19:23.640
Sure.

00:19:32.090 --> 00:19:34.860
I shouldn't keep everyone waiting...

00:19:34.860 --> 00:19:36.030
Yeah.

00:19:36.030 --> 00:19:38.300
Alain, just once more...

00:19:38.300 --> 00:19:41.070
Would it be all right if I cry one last time?

00:19:41.070 --> 00:19:42.970
Yeah, go ahead.

00:19:42.970 --> 00:19:44.160
Let it all out.

00:20:00.550 --> 00:20:04.080
<i>At 1 P.M., the battle had finally begun.</i>

00:20:15.470 --> 00:20:20.640
<i>At this time, the Bastille was only guarded by
114 soldiers led by Marquis de Launay, but...</i>

00:20:20.640 --> 00:20:23.650
<i>...the solid fortress walls and mighty cannons,</i>

00:20:23.650 --> 00:20:28.160
<i>plunged tens of thousands of demonstrators
into the depths of Hell.</i>

00:20:37.420 --> 00:20:38.040
Damn!

00:20:38.960 --> 00:20:40.880
What's going on with our cannons?!

00:20:41.430 --> 00:20:42.600
Why aren't we firing them?!

00:20:42.600 --> 00:20:44.840
We have 12 cannons sitting here!

00:20:45.200 --> 00:20:47.140
We haven't got a clue...

00:20:47.140 --> 00:20:50.560
No one knows how to fire a cannon!

00:20:51.370 --> 00:20:52.210
What?!

00:20:52.210 --> 00:20:53.350
Bernard!

00:20:53.350 --> 00:20:54.510
Oscar...!

00:20:54.510 --> 00:20:56.810
I'm sorry to make you wait!

00:20:57.280 --> 00:20:59.770
We'll take care of the cannons!

00:21:04.360 --> 00:21:06.320
All right! All men, take your positions!

00:21:06.320 --> 00:21:07.320
Prepare the ammunition!

00:21:07.690 --> 00:21:08.810
Yeah!

00:21:18.130 --> 00:21:20.030
Firing angle, 45 degrees!

00:21:20.030 --> 00:21:21.590
Target the upper fortress walls!

00:21:26.870 --> 00:21:27.680
Fire!

00:21:34.560 --> 00:21:35.540
Fire!

00:21:41.260 --> 00:21:42.620
Governor de Launay!

00:21:42.620 --> 00:21:44.220
The enemy has begun firing cannons!

00:21:45.860 --> 00:21:48.700
Furthermore, their aim is quite precise!

00:21:48.700 --> 00:21:50.390
Bastille will fall at this rate!

00:21:55.940 --> 00:21:57.370
Fire!

00:22:00.410 --> 00:22:03.210
All right, all aim for that commander!

00:22:03.210 --> 00:22:04.340
Fire all at once!

00:22:11.690 --> 00:22:12.770
Fire!

